{"name":"Saltwater Denim","hex":"#145c78","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"saltwater","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of salt water."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["New Keynesian or Keynesian, in reference to macroeconomics and economics departments on the East Coast and West Coast of the United States of America."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/saltwater"},{"term":"denim","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A textile often made of cotton with a distinct diagonal pattern."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/denim"}],"entries":[{"title":"Saline water","blocks":[{"text":"Saline water is water that contains a high concentration of dissolved salts. On the United States Geological Survey (USGS) salinity scale, saline water is saltier than brackish water, but less salty than brine. The salt concentration is usually expressed in parts per thousand and parts per million (ppm). The USGS salinity scale defines three levels of saline water. The salt concentration in slightly saline water is 1,000 to 3,000 ppm (0.1–0.3%); in moderately saline water is 3,000 to 10,000 ppm (0.3–1%); and in highly saline water is 10,000 to 35,000 ppm (1–3.5%). Seawater has a salinity of roughly 35,000 ppm, equivalent to 35 grams of salt per one liter of water. The saturation level is only nominally dependent on the temperature of the water. At 20 °C (68 °F) one liter of water can dissolve about 357 grams of salt, a concentration of 26.3 percent by weight. At 100 °C (212 °F), the amount of salt that can be dissolved in one liter of water increases to about 391 grams, a concentration of 28.1% w/w."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Water_salinity_diagram.png/330px-Water_salinity_diagram.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saline_water"},{"title":"Denim","blocks":[{"text":"Denim is a sturdy, cotton, warp-faced textile in which the weft passes under two or more warp threads. This twill weave produces a diagonal ribbing that distinguishes it from cotton duck. Denim, as it is recognised today, was first produced in Nîmes, France."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Denim_texture_03.jpg/330px-Denim_texture_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denim"},{"title":"List of colors: A–F","blocks":[{"text":"The following is a list of colors. A number of the color swatches below are taken from domain-specific naming schemes such as X11 or HTML4. RGB values are given for each swatch because such standards are defined in terms of the sRGB color space. It is not possible to accurately convert many of these swatches to CMYK values because of the differing gamuts of the two spaces, but the color management systems built into operating systems and image editing software attempt such conversions as accurately as possible."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Colouring_pencils.jpg/330px-Colouring_pencils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_colors%3A_A%E2%80%93F"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/WatNaCl.png/500px-WatNaCl.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/WatNaCl.png/1280px-WatNaCl.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/WatNaCl.png/500px-WatNaCl.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Water-NaCl phase diagram","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWatNaCl.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Denim_jacket_cuff_closeup.jpg/500